A||N||3||johnmac||CV boot covers replacement - HOW?||||1||
Z||000000||johnmac||05-04-2001||12:21 AM||||I bought new inner and outer cv joint boot covers.<P>Can I replace them without taking the axle off?<P>What is the procedure?<P>I have a 86 GT Automatic?<P>Thanks,<P>John<P>------------------<BR>John MacHaffie<BR>86GT "Yellowbird"<BR>12+ month project car<BR>johnmac@mpinet.net<BR>Winter Springs, Florida||||reg||1
Z||000001||Coop88||05-04-2001||01:26 AM||||I don't think you can slide the new boot on without removing the output shaft(axle). The haynes manual has the removal procedure. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||05-04-2001||01:51 PM||||Only if you bought the split boots.  An OEM replacement boot slips over the end of the axel after you remove the CV joint.<P>The split boots come with a super glue adhesive to glue the two halves together.||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||05-04-2001||02:22 PM||||Ya, he's right about the split boot, but I wouldn't recomend it unless you want to sell the car. They suck.........leak. Don't buy one of those "one size fits all" boots that you cut down to fit your application either........they also suck. Before you remove the wheels, brakes, struts, be sure to loosen that big axle nut that is accessed through the middle of the rotor. Then pull all that crap apart and slide the axle out. Oh, you say the shaft is retained inside the transaxle? If you have one of those rare applications you need to remove the transaxle from the car and then.........just kidding. Actually, you need to carefully place a screwdriver between the transaxle and Cv joint and lightly give a little pull as you spin the short shaft. There's a little retaining ring inside the transaxle on the shaft that is spring loaded. You have to give a few light tugs to compress it and pull it out.||||reg||1
